{{FRdot|Albi}}
{{commons|Albi}}

'''Albi''' is a town and [[commune in France|commune]] in southern [[France]]. It is the ''[[préfecture]]'' (capital) of the [[Tarn]] ''[[Département in France|département]]'', and is located on the [[Tarn River]], 50 miles northeast of [[Toulouse]]. Its inhabitants are called ''Albigensians'' ([[French language|French]]: ''Albigeois'').

==Administration==

Albi is the chief town of 6 [[Canton in France|cantons]], covering 18 [[Commune in France|communes]], with a total population of 67,728.

==History==

The city was founded by the [[Roman Empire]], and was then known as ''Albiga''. The [[Albigensians]], [[12th century|12th]] and [[13th century]] who created their own variation of Christianity (an outrage to the dominant Catholics), got their name from this city.

==Sights==

Among the monuments of the town are the Sainte Cécile [[cathedral]], the world's largest brick construction, and the Old Bridge (''Pont Vieux'') still in use today after a millennium of existence.

==Miscellaneous==
Albi was the birthplace of:

* [[Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec]] ([[1864]]–[[1901]]), [[painter]]
* [[Gérard Onesta]] (born [[1960]]), politician and [[Member of the European Parliament]]

==External links==

* [http://www.mairie-albi.fr/eng/index.html Albi official Website]
* [http://france-for-visitors.com/languedoc/albi.html Visiting Albi] (English)
* [http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/01267a.htm Archdiocese of Albi (Albia)] - Article from the Catholic Encyclopedia 1908

