  | coordinates = {{coor dms|13|03|17|S|130|54|18|E|type:landmark_region:AU}}
  | area = 1,461.18 km&amp;sup2;
  | established = 1986
  | visitation_num = 
  | visitation_year = 
  | managing_authorities = Parks and Wildlife Commission of the Northern Territory
  | official_site = [http://www.nt.gov.au/ipe/conmgt/index.cfm?fuseaction=viewPark&amp;page=1&amp;menu=ParkRes&amp;pid=85 Litchfield National Park]
}}
'''Litchfield National Park''' is in the [[Northern Territory]] ([[Australia]]), 69 km south of [[Darwin, Northern Territory|Darwin]]. It is named after [[Fred Litchfield]], a Territory pioneer, who explored areas of the Northern Territory from [[Escape Cliffs]] on the [[Timor Sea]] to the [[Daly River]] in [[1864]]. 

The park's main attractions are the waterfalls and the clear pools of water they cascade into. During the hot dry season the park is a magnet for people looking for a refreshing swim. [[Crocodile]]s do not seem to be as much a threat in Litchfield as they are in other Northern Territory parks, such as [[Kakadu National Park]].  

Other features of the park include the [[termite]] mounds and the &quot;Lost City&quot;,  an area of bizarre sandstone block and pillar formations which have been sculpted by wind and rain over thousands of years.

''See also:'' [[Protected areas of the Northern Territory (Australia)]]
{{NorthernTerritory-geo-stub}}

[[Image:IMGP2573_(Small).JPG|left|thumb|400px|Litchfield's Wangi Falls (dry season - April 2004)]]
[[Image:Termite mounds.jpg|left|thumb|400px|Termite mounds in Litchfield National Park.]]

[[Category:National parks of the Northern Territory]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
