      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>translated to es:</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Infobox_protected_area_of_Australia | name = Nitmiluk National Park
  | iucn_category = II
  | image = Australia_Locator_Map.svg
  | caption = 
  | locator_x = 137
  | locator_y = 25
  | nearest_town_or_city  = [[Katherine, Northern Territory|Katherine]]
  | coordinates = {{coor dms|13|54|30|S|132|30|31|E|type:landmark_region:AU}}
  | area = 2,920.99 km&amp;sup2;
  | established = 1989
  | visitation_num = 
  | visitation_year = 
  | managing_authorities = Parks and Wildlife Commission of the Northern Territory&lt;br&gt;Aboriginal traditional land owners (the [[Jawoyn]] people)
  | official_site = [http://www.nt.gov.au/ipe/conmgt/index.cfm?fuseaction=viewPark&amp;page=1&amp;menu=ParkRes&amp;pid=88 Nitmiluk National Park]
}}
'''Nitmiluk National Park''' which is in the [[Northern Territory]] of [[Australia]], 244 km southeast of [[Darwin, Northern Territory|Darwin]], has been established around a series of gorges on the [[Katherine River]] and Edith Falls.

These gorges and the surrounding landscape have great ceremonial significance to the local [[Jawoyn]] people, who are custodians of Nitmiluk National Park. 

The gorges can be explored by canoe and flat bottomed boat.  In the dry season the gorges become separated as the level of the river falls.  They are interconnected in the wet.

There is a  visitor centre located at the [[Katherine Gorge]], about 30 km east of the town of [[Katherine, Northern Territory|Katherine]]. Information displays are set up that explain the geology, landscape and aboriginal history of the National Park.  Tours can be booked at the centre or at the visitor centre in the town. 

''See also:'' [[Protected areas of the Northern Territory (Australia)]]

&lt;gallery&gt;
image:Katherine_Gorge.JPG|Katherine Gorge
