  <page>
    <title>Robots Exclusion Standard</title>
    <id>101673</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41860551</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-02T05:05:07Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Coffeecoder</username>
        <id>1015742</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* External links */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">The '''robots exclusion standard''' or '''robots.txt protocol''' is a convention to prevent cooperating [[web spider]]s and other [[web robot]]s from accessing all or part of a [[website]]. The information specifying the parts that should not be accessed is specified in a file called '''robots.txt''' in the top-level directory of the website.

The robots.txt protocol was [http://www.robotstxt.org/wc/norobots.html created by consensus] in June 1994 by members of the robots mailing list (robots-request@nexor.co.uk).  There is no official standards body or [[RFC]] for the protocol.

The protocol is purely advisory.  It relies on the cooperation of the web robot, so that marking an area of your site out of bounds with robots.txt does not guarantee privacy.  Many web site administrators have been caught trying to use the robots file to make private parts of a website invisible to the rest of the world.  However, the file is necessarily publicly available and is easily checked by anyone with a web browser.

The robots.txt patterns are matched by simple substring comparisons, so care should be taken to make sure that patterns matching directories have the final '/' character appended: otherwise all files with names starting with that substring will match, rather than just those in the directory intended.

==Examples==
This example '''allows all robots''' to visit '''all files''' because the wildcard &quot;*&quot; specifies all robots. 
&lt;pre&gt;
User-agent: *
Disallow:
&lt;/pre&gt;

This example keeps '''all robots out''':
&lt;pre&gt;
User-agent: *
Disallow: /
&lt;/pre&gt;

The next is an example that tells '''all crawlers''' not to enter into four directories of a website:
&lt;pre&gt;
User-agent: *
Disallow: /cgi-bin/
Disallow: /images/
Disallow: /tmp/
Disallow: /private/
&lt;/pre&gt;

Example that tells '''a specific crawler''' not to enter one specific directory:
&lt;pre&gt;
User-agent: BadBot
Disallow: /private/
&lt;/pre&gt;

==Warning==
'''Do not use'''

&lt;pre&gt;
Disallow: *
&lt;/pre&gt;

This is not a stable standard extensions; e.g. although Google [http://www.google.com/webmasters/remove.html claims to support such use], it in fact [http://groups.google.com/groups?q=elvey+googlebot does not]. Use instead:

&lt;pre&gt;
Disallow: /
&lt;/pre&gt;

If you really want to block access to your web site to all robots.

==Alternatives==

robots.txt is older and more widely accepted, but there are other methods (which can be used together with robots.txt) that allow greater control, like disabling indexing of images only or disabling archiving of page contents.

===HTML meta tags for robots===

[[HTML]] [[meta tag]]s can be used to exclude robots according to the contents of web pages. Again, this is purely advisory, and also relies on the cooperation of the robot programs. For example, 

&lt;pre&gt;&lt;meta name=&quot;Robots&quot; content=&quot;Noindex,Nofollow&quot; /&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

within the &lt;code&gt;HEAD&lt;/code&gt; section of an [[HTML]] document tells search engines such as [[Google]], [[Yahoo!]], or [[MSN]] to exclude the page from its index and not to follow any links on this page for further possible indexing.

(See [http://www.robotstxt.org/wc/meta-user.html HTML Author's Guide to the Robots META tag].)

==See also==

* [[Web crawler]]
* [[Spam in blogs#rel=nofollow|The nofollow attribute]]

==External links==
*[http://www.whitehouse.gov/robots.txt White House robots.txt] - 'The robots.txt of the US White House' 
*[http://www.fleiner.com/bots/ Fleiner.com] - 'How to keep bad robots, spiders and web crawlers away'
*[http://www.ilovejackdaniels.com/development/robots-txt-file/ ILoveJackDaniels.com] - 'robots.txt File' (tutorial on how and why to add a robots.txt file to websites, July 19, 2004)
*[http://www.kloth.net/internet/badbots.php List of Bad Bots:] &quot;A short list of bad spiders and nasty bots seen on my different web sites&quot; (Kloth.net) - including those that violate the robots exclusion standard
*[http://laffey.tv/favicon_error_logs.html Laffey.tv] - 'How To: Serving up default favicon.ico and robots.txt files with apache'
*[http://tool.motoricerca.info/robots-checker.phtml Motoricera.info] - 'Robots.txt Checker' (validates robots.txt files and gives optimization tips)
*[http://www.robotstxt.org/wc/norobots.html Robotstxt.org] - 'A Standard for Robot Exclusion'
*[http://www.robotstxt.org/wc/exclusion.html Robotstxt.org] - 'Robots Exclusion'
*[http://www.searchengineworld.com/cgi-bin/robotcheck.cgi SearchEngineWorld.com] - 'Robots.txt Validator' (validates robots.txt files on websites)
*[http://www.searchengineworld.com/robots/robots_tutorial.htm SearchEngineWorld.com] - 'Robots.txt Tutorial'
*[http://www.yellowpipe.com/yis/tools/robots.txt/ Yellowpipe.com] - 'Robots.txt Online Generator'
*[http://www.rietta.com/robogen RoboGen] - Proprietary (shareware license) Windows program for creating and editing robot exclusion files.
*[http://www.theinquirer.net/?article=19357 TheInquirer.net] - 'White House site has oddities, like Bush site: Bender points to possible whitewash', Nick Farrell (October 29, 2004)
*[http://www.cre8asiteforums.com/viewtopic.php?t=8412 Cre8asite Robots.txt Article] - 'Comprehensive Robots.txt Tutorial'
*[http://www.cheap-web-site-hosting.com.au/faq/answers/robots-text.html Controlling Search Engine Robots] - 'Using Robots.txt To Manage Search Engine Spiders'
[[Category:World Wide Web]]
[[de:Robots Exclusion Standard]]
[[nl:robots.txt]]
[[pl:Robots Exclusion Protocol]]
[[ru:robots.txt]]
[[zh:Robots.txt]]
[[fr:Fichier d'exclusion des robots]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
