'''Franklin-Gordon Wild Rivers''' is a national park in [[Tasmania]] ([[Australia]]), 117 km west of [[Hobart, Australia|Hobart]].

== Location ==
The [[Franklin-Gordon Wild Rivers National Park]] lies in the heart of the [[Tasmanian Wilderness]] World Heritage Area. It is a region of dramatic mountain peaks, beautiful [[rainforest]], deep river valleys and spectacular gorges. The park is famous for the wild and pristine rivers that twist their way through the [[wilderness]]. 

== History ==
The genesis of the Wild Rivers National Park was in the earlier [[Frenchman's Cap National Park]] which had the Franklin River as its boundary on the northern and western borders.  [[Frenchman's Cap]] is a dominant feature in the region, and can be seen on the skyline from the west and north of the park.

The Franklin River become synonymous with [[Australia]]'s largest conservation battle - the battle to save the Franklin from the [[Franklin Dam]].

The Dam was  a proposed hydro-electric power scheme that was long in the plans of [[Hydro Tasmania|The Hydro]] - but it was the enthusiastic endorsement by [[Robin Gray (Tasmania)|Robin Gray]]'s Liberal Government who would have flooded the river. It became a national issue. against the [[Tasmanian Wilderness Society]] led by its director [[Bob Brown]].

==Access Points==
The [[Lyell Highway]] winds for 56 kilometres through the heart of the [[Franklin-Gordon Wild Rivers National Park]].

==See also==
* [[Protected areas of Tasmania (Australia)]]

[[Category:National parks of Tasmania]]


{{Tasmania-geo-stub}}

[[nl:Nationaal park Franklin-Gordon Wild Rivers]]
[[ja:フランクリン＝ゴードン・ワイルド・リバーズ国立公園]]</text>
