'''Maria Island''' is a national park in [[Tasmania]] ([[Australia]]), 85 km northeast of [[Hobart, Australia|Hobart]].

==History==
Whalers first settled Maria Island in the early 1800s. Since then it has been home to many operations including a convict settlement and a cement company.

==Accommodation and facilities==
The park has an extensive camping area as well as the converted penitentiary that features nine rooms sleeping up to six in each. There is no electricity or vehicular access and there are no shops on the island. Some rainwater, a public phone, as well as toilets and gas barbecues and showers are provided.

==Sights==
&lt;!-- Unsourced image removed: [[Image:Maria_Island.jpg|thumbnail|Maria Island: view from Bishop and Clerk over fossil cliffs]] --&gt;
Maria Island is popular not only with walker but also bike riders and offers some excellent bike tracks. It is also of historic significance with many ruins and restored historic buildings. Bird watching is popular as the island has a diverse range of wildlife, including the endangered 40 spotted pardalotte and the Cape Barren geese.

*Painted cliffs: The painted cliffs are one of Maria islands most famous attractions. The weathered sandstone cliffs are laced with beautiful mineral patterns.

*Fossil Cliffs: The spectacular cliffs are rich in fossilized shellfish.

*Bishop and Clark: Affording some of the best views of Maria Island, this walk gets steep and rocky toward the end. The location provides a view over the island as well as of the east cost of Tasmania.

==Access==

There are two ferries operating to Maria Island. One departs at Triabunna and the other at the Eastcoaster resort just north of Orford. This is about an hour and a half east of Hobart.

==See also==
*[[Protected areas of Tasmania]]

[[Category:National parks of Tasmania]]

[[nl:Nationaal park Maria Island]]</text>
    </revision>
