'''Mount Field''' is a national park in [[Tasmania]] ([[Australia]]), an hour (64 km) northwest of [[Hobart, Australia|Hobart]]. The landscape ranges from [[eucalyptus]] forest to alpine moorland, rising to 1,434 metres (4,705ft) at the summit of Mount Field West.

==History==
Mount Field was founded in [[1916]], making it, along with [[Freycinet National Park]], Tasmania's oldest national parks.  The last known wild [[thylacine]] was captured in the region in 1933.

==Accommodation &amp; facilities== 
There is camping, powered and unpowered sites, as well as the lake dobsen cabins.

Beside the Visitor center there are Picnic and barbeque facilities as well as a playground and public toilets.

==Sights==
[[Image:Russell_Falls_Tasmania.jpg|thumb|210px|left|Russell Falls in Mt Field National Park]]
Mt Field is one of the most popular national parks in Tasmania as there is a vast variety of things to see and do.

*Russell Falls (10 min - EASY)
One of the most popular scenic attraction in Tasmania, Russell Falls is an easy 10 minute stroll down concrete and boardwalks. 

*Tall Trees Walk  (30 min - EASY)
A walk through the tall [[snow gums]] 

*There are many other walks including to Mt Fields and around the [[Tarn (lake)|tarn]] shelf.
Many of these begin from Lake Dobson.
From the car park, you can do an easy half hour walk around the lake.
For the more enthusiastic, walks can be done to the alpine ski fields and beyond.

A great view of Seal Lake can be had about 10mins from the lodge area.
The walk to the lookout from the car park takes around 2-3 hours as there is
a steep climb to the top.

==See also==
* [[Protected areas of Tasmania (Australia)]]

[[Category:National parks of Tasmania]]

[[de:Mount Field National Park]]
[[nl:Nationaal park Mount Field]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
