}}
'''''The Family Man''''' is a [[2000 in film|2000]] [[Brett Ratner]] film starring [[Nicolas Cage]] and [[Téa Leoni]], about a man who is given a glimpse at what could have been, if he had made a different decision 13 years ago. It is similar to ''[[It's a Wonderful Life]]'', in that it starts on [[Christmas]] Eve with a life and death situation involving an [[angel]] who tries to get the main character to take a long, earnest look at his life.

==Synopsis==
{{spoiler}}
Jack Campbell is a single, wealthy [[Wall Street]] [[stock trader|trader]] living the high life in [[New York City]].  All that magically changes one morning when he wakes up in a suburban [[New Jersey]] bedroom with a wife he never married and two kids he never had &amp;mdash; the life he would have led if he had made different chioces as a younger man.  Jack is a man who wants to get home. But he can not: he is stuck in a &quot;permanent acid trip&quot; because an angel thought he was being cocky (&quot;I've got all I need&quot; / &quot;It must be great being you&quot;) and displaces him into an entirely new world beyond his imaginings: suburban New Jersey.

==External links==
*{{imdb title|id=0218967|title=The Family Man}}

{{fantasy-film-stub}}
[[Category:2000 films|Family Man, The]]
[[Category:American films|Family Man, The]]
[[Category:Films set in New Jersey|Family Man, The]]

[[sv:En andra chans]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Alberti bass</title>
    <id>101819</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41075571</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-24T22:11:59Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Starwiz</username>
        <id>162057</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>Moved text above image and media link</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Alberti bass''' is a particular kind of [[accompaniment]] in [[music]], often used in the [[classical music era]]. It was named after [[Domenico Alberti]] (1710-1740), who used it extensively, although he was not the first to use it.

Alberti bass is a kind of [[broken chord]] or [[arpeggio|arpeggiated]] accompaniment, where the notes of the [[chord (music)|chord]] are presented in the order lowest, highest, middle, highest. This pattern is then repeated.

Alberti bass is usually found in the left hand of pieces for [[keyboard instrument]]s. However, it is sometimes found in pieces for other instruments.  For example [[Béla Bartók]] uses it towards the end of his ''[[String Quartet No. 5 (Bartók)|String Quartet No. 5]]''.

A well known example of Alberti bass is found at the beginning of [[Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart]]'s ''[[Piano Sonata, K. 545 (Mozart)|Piano Sonata, K 545]]'':

[[Image:Mozart k545 opening.png]]

{{multi-listen start}}
{{multi-listen item|filename=K545 allegro.ogg|title=K545, movement 1 - Allegro|description=Converted from [[Mutopia]] public domain MIDI|format=[[Ogg]]}}
{{multi-listen end}}

[[Category:Accompaniment]]
[[de:Alberti-Bass]]
[[fr:Basse d'Alberti]]
[[fi:Albertin basso]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
